00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.980
And then I'll do a little demo.

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:14.690
And then I will hand it over
to Renata and Dimas, who

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.190
will talk about
their application

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.440
that they have built for GOJEK.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.600
And they're also going
to do a short demo.

00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:23.840
We hope to fit all this
into the allocated time

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:26.460
and also leave some
time for questions.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.530
So here's the overview
and trends, right?

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:33.920
So what is driving data
governance initiatives?

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:38.000
A lot of you are very interested
in how to manage your data, how

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.580
to discover your data.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.430
Because we are in a world where
there is no shortage of data,

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:43.160
right?

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:44.060
Data is everywhere.

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:45.060
We are drowning in data.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.610
The question is, how do we
make good use of the data?

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:52.520
How do we find the relevant
data, the right data

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.540
to drive your decisions?

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:59.600
And in the context of things
like GDPR, California Act,

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:02.030
HIPAA, et cetera, how do we
actually govern the data?

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:05.450
How do we make sure that
the right people have access

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:06.680
to the right data?

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.949
And then you can generate
reports, and be compliant,

00:02:09.949 --> 00:02:12.060
and not get in trouble, right?

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.120
So there's the risk
management part of it

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:16.070
that needs to be managed.

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:19.340
There are the operational
efficiencies and other aspects

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:20.720
that you need to tackle.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:23.900
And essentially, if
you do not manage it,

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:25.970
you don't have any
control over your data.

00:02:25.970 --> 00:02:31.620
And data governance is driven
by these bigger requirements.

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.460
So in that context,
Data Catalog gives you

00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:37.520
a framework, a foundation for
building your data governance

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:38.700
services.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.300
And so we have a pretty
aggressive roadmap.

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:44.270
But we start with
data discovery, right?

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:48.180
Because without proper data
discovery, you have nothing,

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.500
and the organizations
need an effective solution

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:53.960
for data discovery, right?

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.820
And the challenge
of data discovery

00:02:55.820 --> 00:02:58.650
is that your data is
not all in one place.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:00.320
It's in disparate places.

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.300
It's in different systems.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:04.280
It's spread all over.

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:05.450
So how an analyst--

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:08.030
let's take the canonical
case of an analyst who

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:10.700
recently joined the
organization and needs

00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:13.520
to figure out what data--

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:15.380
what data they have
access to, and how

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:18.830
they can get access to it, and
how do they make sense of it.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:20.940
And there is a need for
the data discovery tool.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:21.840
So what we have--

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:24.680
this is the context
of why it is needed.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:29.330
So we have built a system that
provides a unified view of all

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:30.080
your data assets.

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.780
And we'll start, initially,
with the data assets in GCP.

00:03:34.780 --> 00:03:36.650
Let's say you have
data assets in GCP

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:40.490
in different sets of, like,
BigQuery, Pub/Sub, GCS,

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:43.220
and going forward, lots
of different systems.

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.400
We give you a way to discover
your data that is spread

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.090
across all these systems.

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:51.020
And it is a smart
catalog in the sense

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:53.090
that you have to
do minimal work.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:56.670
So before I go into that, a
little bit of history right--

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.052
how did we come across this--

00:03:58.052 --> 00:03:59.510
how did we come up
with this notion

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:01.490
of building a data catalog?

00:04:01.490 --> 00:04:04.340
Well, Google has been
dealing with a large number

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:06.110
of data assets for many years.

00:04:06.110 --> 00:04:09.990
And Google felt the need to
build something for itself a

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:10.490
long back.

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:13.610
And we are in our third
iteration of this internal data

00:04:13.610 --> 00:04:16.130
catalog that handles more
than a million objects.

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:19.160
And it's used by more than
30,000 active Googlers.

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:21.110
So we have built a
very scalable system.

00:04:21.110 --> 00:04:24.320
And now we want to take
that underlying technology

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:27.740
and make the data catalog
available to our GCP customers.

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:29.630
So that is the history.

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.320
Now talking about the data
catalog, in addition to

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:34.700
the technical
metadata-- and I'll

00:04:34.700 --> 00:04:37.550
talk about what is-- what we
consider technical metadata

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:41.210
and what we consider business
metadata-- we enable users

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.640
to annotate business
metadata, and that,

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:47.210
too, in a collaborative
manner, to add overall value

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:51.020
and put data intelligence
in the data assets, right?

00:04:51.020 --> 00:04:52.940
And as I mentioned
earlier, Data Catalog

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:56.360
provides the foundation
for data governance, right?

00:04:56.360 --> 00:05:00.210
So Data Catalog was
in alpha for a while.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.060
And we recently announced beta.

00:05:02.060 --> 00:05:06.530
In the current incarnation--
this is the overview, right--

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:10.040
Data Catalog automatically
ingests metadata

00:05:10.040 --> 00:05:13.600
from BigQuery, Pub/Sub, and GCS.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:17.240
And it provides a simple search
interface for data discovery.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:20.960
Your technical users as
well as your business users

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:22.670
can easily access it.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.580
It supports a UI
as well as an API

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.390
so that you can do all kinds
of bulk annotation of data.

00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:32.150
We have the innovation
here in business metadata.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:33.980
Instead of having
simple tags, we

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:37.910
provide schematized tags to
capture really rich business

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:38.480
metadata.

00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:40.470
We'll talk about
that in a moment.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.050
And as I mentioned, we
auto-ingest the data.

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:46.040
Another strong feature, another
feature that I'm very proud of,

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:48.740
is that we have ACL controls
on the business mandate data

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:51.080
that also very strong.

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:54.050
And for technical metadata,
they're actually built in.

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:55.790
They just leverage
the existing ACL.

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:59.900
So you, as a user, have to
do very little extra work.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.330
The last bullet is
very attractive to many

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:05.050
of our customers because
there is a lot of data.

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:08.310
Our customers have thousands
of, let's say, BigQuery tables.

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:10.650
And each has, maybe,
hundreds of columns.

00:06:10.650 --> 00:06:14.670
And it's very hard to
easily identify PII data.

00:06:14.670 --> 00:06:18.210
So we offer an integration
with DLP, Data Loss Prevention

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:21.270
service, which
automatically scans the data

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:23.970
and creates appropriate
tags to identify

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:27.360
sensitive data like PII data.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:30.230
So a little bit about the
Data Catalog architecture

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:31.660
internally--

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.020
you know, if I had to
identify two blocks,

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:36.030
it's like we have
a metadata store.

00:06:36.030 --> 00:06:37.230
It's a transactional store.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.090
And internally, we use Spanner.

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.700
We also have a search
index that we built in.

00:06:41.700 --> 00:06:45.060
The search index leverages
the same technology

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:49.830
that powers Gmail and Google
Drive, which has ACL checks

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:50.760
built into it.

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:54.490
And it's a very scalable
performance system.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:06:56.490
So we know that we can
build a system that

00:06:56.490 --> 00:06:57.900
is really, really scalable.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.080
Now talking about, what
does it do to you as a user,

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:04.740
it makes data discovery
available at your fingertips

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:07.920
without almost any
additional effort.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.250
Because since the data
catalog automatically syncs

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:13.530
all the metadata from
all these various sources

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:17.280
and puts them in index, you
can do a simple keyword search.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:19.950
And anybody can just
find appropriate matches.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:24.630
If you're a power user, it also
enables you to do facet search,

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:28.560
just like you can do for
G Drive or Gmail, right?

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:31.680
So you can say, OK, I want
to look only for table names,

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:34.410
or I want to look only
for certain views,

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:38.160
or I want to look for assets
where the column matches

00:07:38.160 --> 00:07:39.830
a particular keyword.

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:43.350
Or I have created tags
that say, hey, this is PII.

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.983
So show me everything
that matches PII.

00:07:45.983 --> 00:07:47.400
And in the demo,
I will show more.

00:07:47.400 --> 00:07:50.490
But a combination of
simple keyword search

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:53.220
and really powerful
facet search enables

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:58.020
you to do data discovery
very, very easily, all right?

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.880
Now in addition to the
UI, as I mentioned,

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.120
if you have lots
and lots of data,

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:05.660
in order to annotate
them in a effective way,

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:10.440
we enable you to do bulk
updates of metadata through API.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.280
And in beta, we are going to
be supporting Python, Java,

00:08:14.280 --> 00:08:17.010
and Node.js language
libraries to make

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:21.510
it very easy for you to
do the API integration

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:23.950
in a programmatic way.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:27.690
Now API enables our
customers to build

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:31.380
enterprise applications that
are very specific to their need.

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:34.860
It also enables customers
to build their own front

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:39.750
end if they choose to do so, and
use the Data Catalog back end.

00:08:39.750 --> 00:08:42.780
I would like to point out that
GOJEK is one of our customers

00:08:42.780 --> 00:08:45.660
who have worked closely with
us and built such a front end.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:48.090
And they will be on
stage, here with me,

00:08:48.090 --> 00:08:51.920
shortly to do a demo of that.

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:53.570
So I talked briefly--

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:56.210
I kind of referred to technical
and business metadata.

00:08:56.210 --> 00:08:58.310
And since it's kind of
central to my discussion,

00:08:58.310 --> 00:09:00.410
I want to spend just
a few seconds talking

00:09:00.410 --> 00:09:03.620
about our notion,
our terminology.

00:09:03.620 --> 00:09:06.740
What is technical metadata, and
what is business metadata, OK?

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:10.040
So technical metadata is
things like table names, data

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:13.280
set name, column names, table
descriptions, date created,

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:16.190
date modified, et cetera,
the metadata that is already

00:09:16.190 --> 00:09:17.510
there in the source system.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:19.760
That, we refer to as
technical metadata.

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.520
And as I mentioned
earlier, we automatically

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:25.610
sink that metadata from the
source system into Data Catalog

00:09:25.610 --> 00:09:27.240
and put that in the index.

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.810
So if you create a new table
in BigQuery, within, like,

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:32.250
three seconds, that
metadata shows up.

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:34.580
There is no need
for you, as a user,

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:37.290
to manually register
these data assets, right?

00:09:37.290 --> 00:09:38.550
So that is automatic.

00:09:38.550 --> 00:09:42.290
But in addition to that, there
is a lot of business context

00:09:42.290 --> 00:09:44.990
that needs to be attached
to the data, things like,

00:09:44.990 --> 00:09:48.170
well, does this
data asset have PII?

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:50.750
Or who is the data owner?

00:09:50.750 --> 00:09:52.550
Does it have a delete
by requirement,

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:53.990
delete by certain date?

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:56.690
Or does it have retain till
certain date requirement?

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:59.080
What is the data
quality score, right?

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:00.110
What is the cardinality?

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:02.600
There is all kinds of
other relevant information

00:10:02.600 --> 00:10:04.340
that needs to be stored.

00:10:04.340 --> 00:10:07.460
And without a proper system
to store this metadata,

00:10:07.460 --> 00:10:09.620
our customers tell
us that, well, they

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:13.160
leave it in some wiki
page, some confluent page,

00:10:13.160 --> 00:10:14.930
or some spreadsheet.

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:17.180
And it's not very searchable.

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:19.380
And it's not very usable.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:22.080
Did I accidentally click that?

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:22.720
OK, sorry.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:33.490
So-- I am having
some clicker issues.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:35.060
All right.

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:38.380
Now here's example, on the
right side, of what the business

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:39.980
metadata looks like, right?

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:42.500
So the business metadata
is schematized tags,

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:43.760
as I mentioned.

00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:47.030
Unlike other data catalogs
where the business tags can

00:10:47.030 --> 00:10:48.830
be simple text
strings, we allow you

00:10:48.830 --> 00:10:52.460
to capture really rich
business metadata using schema.

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:55.100
We believe that, just like
when you have complex data,

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:57.440
you need schema to manage it.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:59.300
Since you have
complex metadata, we

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:01.820
give you the ability to create
schema for your metadata.

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:04.190
We call them templates
so a template can

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:05.900
have multiple fields,
and you can have--

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:07.880
I don't know if you can
read this from here.

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:10.820
You can basically
attach information like,

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:14.190
is this data asset
approved for use?

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:17.030
What is the-- does it have PII?

00:11:17.030 --> 00:11:18.710
What is the type of PII?

00:11:18.710 --> 00:11:22.340
And there are currently five
data types that we support.

00:11:22.340 --> 00:11:23.870
We support string.

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:27.950
We support double, Boolean,
enumerated, and datetime.

00:11:27.950 --> 00:11:29.690
And using these
five types, you can

00:11:29.690 --> 00:11:31.550
create really rich templates.

00:11:31.550 --> 00:11:35.330
And you can use these templates
to create individual tags

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:38.130
that you can attach to
individual data assets.

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:41.180
The next slide is sort
of an illustration.

00:11:41.180 --> 00:11:43.820
For example, let's assume
that you have this thought

00:11:43.820 --> 00:11:45.680
example of a customer table.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:48.440
And you have certain
columns in that table.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:50.690
Wouldn't it be
great if you could

00:11:50.690 --> 00:11:54.070
have some sort of a sticky
note, an electronic sticky note

00:11:54.070 --> 00:11:57.260
if I may, some sort of
a schematized tag that

00:11:57.260 --> 00:11:59.400
has somehow captured all
this rich information?

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:01.220
For example, the common
thing that analysts

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:02.887
do when they come to
work in the morning

00:12:02.887 --> 00:12:05.210
is say, OK, I need
to generate a report.

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:06.830
I need to know if
the last night's ETL

00:12:06.830 --> 00:12:08.570
job ran successfully.

00:12:08.570 --> 00:12:10.070
I wish somebody
could tell me, and I

00:12:10.070 --> 00:12:11.540
didn't have to call
data engineering as to,

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:14.030
hey, what was the status of
the last night's ETL job?

00:12:14.030 --> 00:12:15.800
How many rows were ingested?

00:12:15.800 --> 00:12:16.850
Did we have any errors?

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:18.750
Did we have any warnings?

00:12:18.750 --> 00:12:22.320
Now imagine there was similar
tag, the data governance,

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:24.800
that told you that--

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:26.240
what is the data classification?

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:27.050
Is it public?

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:27.680
Is it private?

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:28.810
Is it sensitive?

00:12:28.810 --> 00:12:30.860
Where, in the
lifecycle, is this data?

00:12:30.860 --> 00:12:31.820
Is it in prod?

00:12:31.820 --> 00:12:32.660
Is it in test?

00:12:32.660 --> 00:12:33.590
Is it deprecated?

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:34.370
To be deleted?

00:12:34.370 --> 00:12:35.420
Where does it stand?

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:38.150
What is the data
quality tag, right?

00:12:38.150 --> 00:12:40.040
What is the completeness
of this data?

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:42.740
What is the freshness
of this data, right?

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:44.540
Does it actually
meet the criteria?

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:46.610
Or has it fallen
off, and somehow I

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:48.650
should look at it with
suspicion, saying,

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:50.540
maybe this is not
the right data?

00:12:50.540 --> 00:12:53.510
We allow you to create
such schematized tags

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:56.510
to capture all this rich
business metadata so that when

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:59.720
you come across a data asset, it
gives you the necessary context

00:12:59.720 --> 00:13:01.640
to use it in a meaningful way.

00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:05.100
We also enable you to create
tags at a column level.

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:08.240
So you can say, for these
columns, the PII is true,

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:09.950
and this is the type of PII.

00:13:09.950 --> 00:13:11.913
This column might
have some funny name,

00:13:11.913 --> 00:13:12.830
but it's actually SSN.

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:14.930
We are using Social
Security number.

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:16.312
This has email address.

00:13:16.312 --> 00:13:17.270
There is another thing.

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:21.470
For example, oftentimes you
come across a data, a column,

00:13:21.470 --> 00:13:23.950
and say, OK, how am I
supposed to use this?

00:13:23.950 --> 00:13:25.710
How was it calculated?

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:28.022
For example, in this case,
we say lifetime value,

00:13:28.022 --> 00:13:29.355
lifetime value of your customer.

00:13:29.355 --> 00:13:32.000
It's like, OK, what was the
formula used to calculate it?

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:33.710
How shall I interpret that?

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.980
All that rich business
metadata that you need in order

00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:39.470
to really leverage
your data asset

00:13:39.470 --> 00:13:41.810
now can be actually put
right next to the data.

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:43.340
And it becomes searchable.

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:46.730
So you can search
by saying, show me

00:13:46.730 --> 00:13:49.460
everything that has PII access.

00:13:49.460 --> 00:13:53.240
Now when I talk about discovery,
of one of the things that

00:13:53.240 --> 00:13:56.770
is really, really important is
that who has access to what,

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:57.320
right?

00:13:57.320 --> 00:13:59.120
In many traditional
data catalogs,

00:13:59.120 --> 00:14:01.760
you have to actually
register your data set.

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:04.010
And then you had to set a
separate set of permissions,

00:14:04.010 --> 00:14:05.630
separate set of ACLs.

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:08.862
What we have done is we say,
OK, you don't need to do that.

00:14:08.862 --> 00:14:10.820
First of all, we do
auto-ingestion of metadata.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:12.860
And then we honor
the same ACLs that

00:14:12.860 --> 00:14:15.360
govern data assets in
your source system.

00:14:15.360 --> 00:14:18.830
So in this example,
we have two users.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:22.430
User 1 has, say, read access
to all data sets in BQ.

00:14:22.430 --> 00:14:26.120
And user 2 has read access to
only the first three data sets,

00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:28.880
has metadata read
access to the fourth

00:14:28.880 --> 00:14:32.090
the data set, and no access
to the fifth data set.

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:34.260
The view that user 2
has in Data Catalog

00:14:34.260 --> 00:14:38.420
is that they can discover
A, B, C, and D. When

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:40.640
they click on the D data
asset though, they won't

00:14:40.640 --> 00:14:42.400
be able to see the actual data.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:44.570
When it comes to the
last data asset, E,

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:46.130
since they do not
have any access,

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:49.460
Data Catalog's search
actually does ACL checks.

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:52.730
And when you run the search, in
that 500 milliseconds that we

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:55.640
try to return results, we
probably run 100,000-plus ACL

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:58.422
checks to say, this
user should see what.

00:14:58.422 --> 00:14:59.630
What are they allowed to see?

00:14:59.630 --> 00:15:02.580
And we show them the
right data assets.

00:15:02.580 --> 00:15:05.090
So you do not need to
do any additional ACLs.

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:07.910
And there is no data
exfiltration of data--

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:10.170
or metadata exfiltration
of any kind.

00:15:10.170 --> 00:15:12.980
So your data is
safe in that sense.

00:15:12.980 --> 00:15:15.320
The ACL checks that
I talk about also

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:17.960
apply to the business
metadata tags.

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:19.950
So in this case,
in this example,

00:15:19.950 --> 00:15:21.200
we have three sets of tags.

00:15:21.200 --> 00:15:23.630
We have an ETL tag, a
data governance tag,

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:25.170
and a data quality tag.

00:15:25.170 --> 00:15:28.040
Now data governance
is able to create tags

00:15:28.040 --> 00:15:29.750
that only they can see.

00:15:29.750 --> 00:15:32.150
Because oftentimes, they put
very sensitive information

00:15:32.150 --> 00:15:33.370
in that metadata.

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:34.930
And maybe they do
not want everybody

00:15:34.930 --> 00:15:36.320
to be able to see that.

00:15:36.320 --> 00:15:39.940
So our metadata tags, the
business tags are also ACL.

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:44.870
Now many of our
customers come to us

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:46.280
and say, oh, this is great.

00:15:46.280 --> 00:15:49.040
And they told us that,
at the same time--

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:54.050
even though it's programmatic,
and it has all these smarts

00:15:54.050 --> 00:15:57.290
our customers realized there's
lots of data where the column

00:15:57.290 --> 00:16:00.000
names are not correct.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.880
Column might say
column 17 or something,

00:16:01.880 --> 00:16:03.710
but actually, it has PII data.

00:16:03.710 --> 00:16:04.890
What should we do?

00:16:04.890 --> 00:16:07.460
So we have worked with DLP
to provide the integration

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:08.990
that I referred to earlier.

00:16:08.990 --> 00:16:11.960
And using that, you can
use DLP to scan your data.

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:15.290
Either you can do full scan,
or you can do sample scan.

00:16:15.290 --> 00:16:19.100
And the integration enables
you to leave the data as-is,

00:16:19.100 --> 00:16:20.240
but attach--

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:22.460
the DLP will automatically
scan and attach

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:25.740
the metadata that will
identify your data to say, OK,

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:28.490
this is PII data.

00:16:28.490 --> 00:16:31.670
And the type of PII is,
let's say, email address.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:34.760
And this is my confidence level,
like 99% certain that this

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:36.470
is the kind of
data, which allows

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:38.330
you to process large
amounts of data

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:41.990
automatically and identify
PII information, which can

00:16:41.990 --> 00:16:43.970
lead to better data governance.

00:16:43.970 --> 00:16:45.410
Because then you
can say, OK, who

00:16:45.410 --> 00:16:49.160
should have access to it, why
is this accessible to everybody,

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:53.120
and then do the
necessary controls.

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:54.830
A brief-- this thing
about pricing--

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:59.630
we wanted to make Data Catalog
very accessible, eminently

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:01.820
accessible to all our users.

00:17:01.820 --> 00:17:03.860
And at the same
time, we didn't want

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:06.680
to make it entirely free for
various reasons-- that it

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:08.250
will be abused, et cetera.

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.310
So our pricing is--

00:17:10.310 --> 00:17:13.940
as I mentioned here,
it's $100 per gigabyte

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:17.540
per month of stored metadata
that is business metadata.

00:17:17.540 --> 00:17:19.670
There is no charge for
technical metadata.

00:17:19.670 --> 00:17:22.579
Because technical metadata
resides in the source systems.

00:17:22.579 --> 00:17:25.940
And the way we look at it,
you have already paid for it.

00:17:25.940 --> 00:17:27.710
You don't want to charge again.

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:31.220
Also, we give you 1 megabyte
of business metadata free so

00:17:31.220 --> 00:17:32.930
that you can try things out.

00:17:32.930 --> 00:17:36.540
In terms of API pricing,
there is a second dimension.

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:40.490
And we have 1 million
Catalog API calls, free

00:17:40.490 --> 00:17:41.730
included for every user.

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:45.270
And we believe most users
would not need more than that.

00:17:45.270 --> 00:17:48.110
But in the off chance that you
are developing some application

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:52.430
that calls lots of API,
it's like $10 per 100,000

00:17:52.430 --> 00:17:53.400
API per month.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:56.060
So it's a very
attractive pricing.

00:17:56.060 --> 00:17:58.220
We are working
closely with a number

00:17:58.220 --> 00:18:01.180
of partners,
Collibra, Informatica,

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:03.710
Tableau, and Looker.

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:06.560
And they will be using our API.

00:18:06.560 --> 00:18:09.550
So I mentioned the
APIs are open and free.

00:18:09.550 --> 00:18:11.940
We believe that
it's your metadata,

00:18:11.940 --> 00:18:13.760
you can do whatever
you want to do with it.

00:18:13.760 --> 00:18:17.210
And using those APIs, they
will be providing integration

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:20.510
in future so that your
on-prem metadata can also

00:18:20.510 --> 00:18:23.360
be ingested into Data
Catalog so that you

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:26.870
will have one catalog to
find all your data assets.

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:29.280
And you won't have to
go to multiple places.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:33.110
So at this point, I'd like to
switch over to a brief demo.

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:36.380
So I wanted to show you what
the Data Catalog looks like.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:37.980
It's early.

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:39.180
We are close to beta.

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:40.130
We are not there yet.

00:18:40.130 --> 00:18:41.900
In the next few
weeks, hopefully we

00:18:41.900 --> 00:18:43.920
will update some
of these things.

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:46.520
But I want to show you how
Data Catalog looks like.

00:18:46.520 --> 00:18:48.500
This will show up
in your console.

00:18:48.500 --> 00:18:51.860
And any of you, if you want to
start accessing Data Catalog

00:18:51.860 --> 00:18:54.110
today, I can whitelist you.

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:56.720
And you can start using
the alpha that's available.

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:59.540
So the Data Catalog
UI looks as follows.

00:18:59.540 --> 00:19:02.380
It's organized along
the lines of cards.

00:19:02.380 --> 00:19:03.380
We have different cards.

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:05.840
There is a card for
exploring data assets.

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:07.670
And each of these
links are clickable.

00:19:07.670 --> 00:19:11.740
And you can just click them
to look for data assets.

00:19:11.740 --> 00:19:13.160
And you can go back here.

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:15.142
You can create
your tag templates.

00:19:15.142 --> 00:19:16.600
You can explore
your tag templates.

00:19:16.600 --> 00:19:18.320
You can create
your tag templates.

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.090
There is search tips.

00:19:20.090 --> 00:19:23.360
You can search using
tags, asset types, column,

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:24.810
bucket names, as I mentioned.

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:26.210
It's a rich facet search.

00:19:26.210 --> 00:19:28.760
And there is a link to
an online documentation.

00:19:28.760 --> 00:19:31.580
On the left side, we
show the popular tables.

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:33.800
In its current incarnation,
it says, OK, we'll

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:36.260
look at the usage,
say, in BigQuery.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:38.350
And which are the
really popular tables

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:40.100
we want to show you
so that you don't even

00:19:40.100 --> 00:19:41.910
have to search for this?

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:43.670
We plan to develop
it in this manner,

00:19:43.670 --> 00:19:46.280
that, in future, it
will be more customized.

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:49.010
My goal is that 50% the
time, you shouldn't even

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:51.350
have to go for the search box.

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:53.685
You should say, oh, how
did this read my mind?

00:19:53.685 --> 00:19:55.310
This is exactly what
I was looking for.

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:57.560
The other 50% of
the time, maybe you

00:19:57.560 --> 00:20:01.070
go search for the data
assets that you want.

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:03.000
Briefly, I want to
show you, for example,

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:05.270
if I look for all
tables and views,

00:20:05.270 --> 00:20:08.840
and I-- so did that
too quickly, I guess.

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:11.360
So we have all these
search results here

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:12.650
that match the table.

00:20:12.650 --> 00:20:14.480
I haven't even
looked for anything

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:16.550
in particular in
terms of keyword.

00:20:16.550 --> 00:20:18.380
But I found this table.

00:20:18.380 --> 00:20:20.270
And this is in Data Catalog.

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:21.500
It shows me the schema.

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:23.670
It shows me the details.

00:20:23.670 --> 00:20:26.360
And there is an integration
built in with BigQuery.

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:29.600
So from Data Catalog, if you
like that particular table,

00:20:29.600 --> 00:20:32.090
you click on it, it
launches BigQuery.

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:33.630
And you can start querying.

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:35.330
You can look at the preview.

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:38.030
And you can-- this is the
integration we provide.

00:20:38.030 --> 00:20:48.310
But in addition-- in addition,
if I say bookings, let's say,

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:50.140
I found a table.

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:52.950
And in this table, I have all
these tags, the kind of tags

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:54.090
that I talked about.

00:20:54.090 --> 00:20:57.300
We have the data discovery
tag, data governance tag,

00:20:57.300 --> 00:20:59.130
quality tag, template tag.

00:20:59.130 --> 00:21:01.560
So let's look at the
data governance tag.

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:03.900
This sort of illustrates
how I can actually

00:21:03.900 --> 00:21:05.100
categorize the data.

00:21:05.100 --> 00:21:06.330
And I say--

00:21:06.330 --> 00:21:09.390
I have the data classification
as an enumerated type.

00:21:09.390 --> 00:21:11.490
And I can choose
the category to say,

00:21:11.490 --> 00:21:14.910
is it public, sensitive,
confidential, regulatory.

00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:17.040
I can specify the
data lifecycle.

00:21:17.040 --> 00:21:18.030
Is it in prep?

00:21:18.030 --> 00:21:18.960
Is it in test?

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:20.690
Is it in prod, et cetera.

00:21:20.690 --> 00:21:24.090
And I can specify all kinds
of rich metadata tags,

00:21:24.090 --> 00:21:25.710
as I talked about, right?

00:21:25.710 --> 00:21:28.140
The other thing that I
wanted to demonstrate

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:30.700
is how we do ACL controls.

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:32.760
So in this case, I
have two windows.

00:21:32.760 --> 00:21:34.710
And here, I'm logged
in as an admin

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:36.360
who has access to everything.

00:21:36.360 --> 00:21:38.700
And here, I'm logged in
as an analyst, who has

00:21:38.700 --> 00:21:41.140
access to only limited things.

00:21:41.140 --> 00:21:44.730
And in this case, when
I look for bookings,

00:21:44.730 --> 00:21:50.010
I should find the
same set of tables.

00:21:50.010 --> 00:21:52.740
I should find the same
set of tables, right?

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:57.600
On the other hand, if
I go to, say, finance,

00:21:57.600 --> 00:22:02.040
I find these two tables here,
and these two tables here.

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:05.220
Now in this case, when
I click on this table

00:22:05.220 --> 00:22:07.260
and try to access
the data, I should

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:10.080
be able to access the data,
because I do have permission.

00:22:10.080 --> 00:22:11.290
I can see this data.

00:22:11.290 --> 00:22:12.840
I can preview this data.

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:16.020
In the other case,
I'm an analyst.

00:22:16.020 --> 00:22:17.350
I can discover this data.

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:18.690
I can discover the metadata.

00:22:18.690 --> 00:22:22.020
But if I actually try
to access the data,

00:22:22.020 --> 00:22:24.330
I should get an error.

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:25.860
I see this error message.

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:27.600
And I cannot preview it.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:31.120
Now that shows that
the ACLs are working.

00:22:31.120 --> 00:22:33.930
If, similarly, I go
to the data asset

00:22:33.930 --> 00:22:37.371
that I do not have access to--

00:22:37.371 --> 00:22:39.720
as an admin, I have
access to HR data.

00:22:39.720 --> 00:22:41.250
I see all this.

00:22:41.250 --> 00:22:44.440
As an analyst, I go to HR data.

00:22:44.440 --> 00:22:46.770
Right, I don't have
access to HR data.

00:22:46.770 --> 00:22:49.015
That just quickly
shows the two things

00:22:49.015 --> 00:22:50.140
that I wanted to highlight.

00:22:50.140 --> 00:22:54.590
One is the schematized metadata,
the tags that we create.

00:22:54.590 --> 00:22:56.850
And the second
thing is the ACLs.

